WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
What do you think of when you hear about&nbsp;
Swaziland? Sugar monarchy, right? But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:09.120
could you tell me where it's located? Many will&nbsp;
say: somewhere south of the African continent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.560
So the fact that looking at a map we can't&nbsp;
immediately be precise about its exact location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.560
tells us that we are looking at a very small state&nbsp;
(just 17,363 sq. km. in area), if not the smallest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.640
in southern Africa and with few inhabitants&nbsp;
(about 1.16 million). Its territory is landlocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:28.080
and shares borders almost entirely with South&nbsp;
Africa and minimally to the east with Mozambique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.600
We can divide it into four distinct regions,&nbsp;
locally called Veld (Afrikaans for "fields"),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.520
the mountainous High-Veld in the northwest (where&nbsp;
the two capitals Mbabane and Lobamba are located),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.640
the hilly Middle-Veld in the center, the flat&nbsp;
Low-Veld in the south, and the Lubombo region&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.680
in the east. The territory is crossed by many&nbsp;
rivers and streams, which provide the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.600
with one of the most abundant water supplies in&nbsp;
southern Africa. Swaziland, or rather eSwatini,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.600
so named by the ruler Mswati III in 2018 on the&nbsp;
occasion of his birthday, isn't only part of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.440
territory of very ancient formation geologically,&nbsp;
but its population history is also ancient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.880
as artifacts and human remains dating back more&nbsp;
than one hundred thousand years have been found.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.400
The Swazis were part of the great migratory&nbsp;
wave of Bantu-speaking Nguni peoples who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:24.800
during the 14th and 15th centuries descended from&nbsp;
the headwaters of the Cross River in Cameroon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.520
through Kenya and Tanzania, to an area between&nbsp;
the Lubombo Mountains and the Indian Ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.880
near Delagoa Bay in Mozambique. And it was in&nbsp;
that area that, in the last decades of the 16th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.000
century, Portuguese sailors shipwrecked off the&nbsp;
coast of Mozambique came across them, claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.680
that the inland areas beyond Delagoa Bay were well&nbsp;
populated and that a brisk ivory trade was taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.560
place along the coast. When clans broke away from&nbsp;
the mother group, they took the name of the clan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.400
leader, and one of the first to do so was that of&nbsp;
Dlamini, the ancestor of the present Swazi royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.280
family. His immediate successors incorporated&nbsp;
many of the other clans along the Lubombo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:07.360
Mountains and began to spread south and west,&nbsp;
eventually settling near what is now Nhlangano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.880
in the southwestern part of present-day eSwatini.&nbsp;
The kingdom then experienced a major expansion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:20.240
thanks to the fighting king Mswati II, who was in&nbsp;
office from 1839 to 1865 and who, taking advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.600
of the peaceful relatio ns his father Sobhuza I&nbsp;
had established with the belligerent Zulu people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.520
from South Africa, was able to extend his holdings&nbsp;
almost undisturbed, incorporating territories such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:38.240
as present-day Nelspruit, Barberton, Carolina,&nbsp;
Ermelo, Piet Retief and Mkhuze. But then came the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.760
turn to reckon with European settlers, and I am&nbsp;
referring to the Boers of the Transvaal Republic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.280
who, in an attempt to free themselves from the&nbsp;
British and desperate for an outlet to the sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:55.200
expanded eastward, trying to wrest land from the&nbsp;
Swazis by hook or crook. Until, with the Pretoria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:01.680
Convention of 1881, the Boers succeeded in forcing&nbsp;
the then Swazi ruler Mbandzeni to accept that at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.640
least half of the original Swazi territory&nbsp;
would fall under Boer hands. At that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:13.280
the lion of Swaziland, King Sobhuza II, in office&nbsp;
since December 1921 was the only one who tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:18.640
to reunite the people divided by those boundary&nbsp;
decisions and cancel the country's existing land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:24.240
property model. It, in fact, didn't grant the&nbsp;
Swazis any control over the economic destiny of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:30.720
the nation, because by granting Boer and Afrikaner&nbsp;
concessionaires mineral deposits and land areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.680
it effectively stripped the country of&nbsp;
sovereignty over those lands. Sobhuza II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.360
in order to have that property model dismantled,&nbsp;
led a delegation to London in 1922 to meet with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.080
King George V, but the British government&nbsp;
declared that it was unwilling to reopen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.280
the issue of African land partition. Of the same&nbsp;
opinion were the Privy Council judges who said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.160
that the measure under which the Swazi land had&nbsp;
been expropriated had the same status as an act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.360
of state and as such couldn't be challenged.&nbsp;
The only solution was to set up a public fund,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:06.800
the "Lifa Fund" to try to slowly buy back the&nbsp;
land previously taken by Boers and Afrikaners.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.800
This necessity failed when the United Kingdom&nbsp;
finally granted independence to Swaziland in 1968.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.440
Then, once independence was achieved and the&nbsp;
land taken away from the concessionaires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.080
it remained to adjust the borders with South&nbsp;
Africa. For the sake of brevity, I will not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:28.000
go into detail and simply tell you that this&nbsp;
wasn t simple and involved several authorities:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.680
Buthelezi, leader of the Kwazulu-Natal province,&nbsp;
the historical home of the Zulus; Pieter Koornhof,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.320
South African minister for cooperation and&nbsp;
development; and the Organization for African&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:44.800
Unity, which was formed in 1963 to eliminate all&nbsp;
forms of colonialism in Africa. It all revolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:50.160
around the unification of Swaziland with two&nbsp;
strategic regions to which Sobhuza II aspired:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:56.320
Kangwane and Ingwavuma, the latter in particular&nbsp;
guaranteeing control of the port city of Khosi Bay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:02.080
and thus an important outlet to the sea. Today's&nbsp;
borders do not correspond to those so desired by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:08.080
Sobhuza II, because the two regions today are part&nbsp;
of South Africa. What interests us is that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:13.760
king decided to remain part of the Commonwealth&nbsp;
and adopted a form of constitutional monarchy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:20.160
Because of this he encountered opposition from&nbsp;
supporters of absolutism, so much so that in 1973,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:25.600
after a long series of clashes, he was forced&nbsp;
to dissolve parliament and political parties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.720
restoring the traditional regime. From there&nbsp;
on, until the ascension in 1986 (April 25) of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.320
the current ruler Mswati III, the triumph of&nbsp;
absolutism would be overwhelming. And any popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:42.160
demands about greater democratic openings would&nbsp;
sometimes be bloodily suppressed and sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.760
silenced with promises that were never kept.&nbsp;
Prominent among these were the tensions and unrest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.480
triggered during 2021 by the killing&nbsp;
by security forces of student Thabani&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:57.760
Nkomonye while participating in a pro-democracy&nbsp;
demonstration. The ruler was accused of hiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.880
foreign mercenaries for the occasion, while the&nbsp;
overall toll of the crackdown was 80 deaths over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:08.400
the course of the various riots. But when you&nbsp;
hold absolute power, it s easy to crush dissent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.960
especially if the people continue to remain&nbsp;
attached to the monarchical institution and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.640
the values of unity and social peace they believe&nbsp;
it represents. Another example of how risky it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:24.320
to challenge the crown's choices is provided by&nbsp;
a 2014 episode. The sentencing to two years in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:29.440
prison of journalist Bheki Makhubu and human&nbsp;
rights lawyer Thulani Maseko, who were guilty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:34.080
of daring to publish an article in a national&nbsp;
newspaper questioning the administration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.200
justice in the country. An attitude so unwelcome&nbsp;
to the top leadership that they also charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.240
them with contempt of court, causing them to be&nbsp;
subjected to pre-trial detention for three months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:17.520
On the day of sentencing, Human Rights Watch&nbsp;
publicly reported that the full exercise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:23.920
human rights in eSwatini was in steep decline, as&nbsp;
political parties aren t allowed and the judiciary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:29.040
is severely compromised, pointing the finger&nbsp;
at the indifference of neighboring South Africa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.200
and the Southern African Development&nbsp;
Community (SADC), of which eSwatini is a part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.360
The same article states that "the government&nbsp;
also (allegedly) abused the repressive tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.400
contained in the 2008 anti-terrorism&nbsp;
law to target independent organizations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.280
accusing them of being 'terrorist' groups,&nbsp;
and crush civil society activists through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.640
abusive surveillance and illegal searches of&nbsp;
homes and offices." And on the press freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:58.640
side? Worse than ever! Broadcaster licenses aren t&nbsp;
recognized except in rare cases, and the two main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:04.000
daily newspapers are related to the government&nbsp;
elite-such as The Eswatini Observer, wholly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:09.120
owned by the omnipresent national investment&nbsp;
fund Tibiyo Taka Ngwane, which among other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:15.120
things is the major shareholder in the sugar giant&nbsp;
Royal Eswatini Sugar Corporation. We ll return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:19.840
this in a moment. Another phenomenon that has&nbsp;
plagued the country for quite some time is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:25.680
political but health-related, namely the very high&nbsp;
incidence of HIV among the population. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:30.240
to available data (UN agency UNAIDS) more than&nbsp;
27 percent of adults are living with the disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:36.160
and in 1999 King Mswati III officially declared&nbsp;
the spread of the virus a national disaster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:41.680
A first move was to enact the recently abolished&nbsp;
Chastity Law, which imposed virginity for women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:47.840
until the age of 24. Efforts were also made to&nbsp;
raise funds for prevention, care and treatment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:55.120
but the resources set aside between 2013 and 2014&nbsp;
were no more than $33 million, less than one-third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.120 --> 00:09:00.880
of the total figure of $100 million allocated by&nbsp;
various international entities. And now we come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.920
to talk about eSwatini from an economic point of&nbsp;
view: we are facing one of the poorest countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:11.040
in the world, with a national GDP of $3.9 billion&nbsp;
(2020 data) that places it at the bottom of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:17.520
world ranking, 160th out of 197. Despite this,&nbsp;
GDP per capita (at $3415 again in 2020) is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:23.280
among the lowest on the African continent, taking&nbsp;
into account that it has almost halved from 2012&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.680
values ($5,719) and that more than half of&nbsp;
the inhabitants live below the poverty line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.040
The rural context occupies the vast majority&nbsp;
of the population, and in fact the predominant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:38.080
economic activity remains agriculture,&nbsp;
while only the large plantations of cotton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:44.720
tobacco but especially sugarcane are managed on an&nbsp;
entrepreneurial basis. Most of the national GDP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:51.040
however, comes from valuable mining activities,&nbsp;
such as diamonds, asbestos, tin, and coal, largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:56.240
controlled by South Africa, while tourism is&nbsp;
experiencing some development, attracted by parks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:02.000
and nature reserves. In fact, these are populated&nbsp;
by extremely rich wildlife, and one piece of news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:08.000
that may not displease animal rights activists is&nbsp;
that under the law of the realm, park rangers can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:13.600
shoot poachers on sight and possibly kill them&nbsp;
without incurring any conviction or penalty. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:19.760
why is eSwatini referred to as the sugar monarchy.&nbsp;
Well simple! because the best, closest to water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:26.320
sources and most fertile lands have been allocated&nbsp;
to intensive sugarcane cultivation. In 2014 there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:31.360
were nearly (385.4) 400 billion liters of water&nbsp;
used by major companies active in the sector to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:36.640
irrigate the thousands of hectares of plantations&nbsp;
located mainly in the Lubombo region. First among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:41.920
them is the Royal Eswatini Sugar Corporation,&nbsp;
which manages nearly twenty-two thousand hectares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:47.120
and produces over four hundred thirty thousand&nbsp;
(437) tons of sugar each year. Its shareholders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:52.960
are the omnipresent Tibiyo Taka Ngwane fund&nbsp;
(53.1%), set up by King Sobhuza II in 1968,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:56.400
RCL foods (29.1%), the government&nbsp;
of Nigeria (10%), the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.800
of Swaziland (6.5%) and others (1.3%). In&nbsp;
second place is Ubombo Sugar Limited, 40%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:07.520
owned by the usual sovereign wealth fund Tibiyo&nbsp;
and 60% by the South African multinational Illovo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:14.640
Sugar Limited. Its consumption of water resources&nbsp;
is no joke either: between 2013 and 2014 it stood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:16.800
at around (source: Water 2014 Information Request&nbsp;
document) one hundred fifty four (153.7) billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:22.480
liters. The problem is that water is not only&nbsp;
consumed, it's often altered, spoiled by intensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:27.760
agriculture that demands much more from the land&nbsp;
than it can provide and by the use of pesticides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:32.880
and fertilizers. But there is little we can&nbsp;
do about it, sugar is the country's flagship:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:38.800
its importance is reflected in exports, GDP and&nbsp;
the number of people employed in "agriculture".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:43.920
Factors, however, that haven t helped to improve&nbsp;
living conditions in eSwatini, given that as I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:49.200
mentioned, GDP per capita is just over $3,000&nbsp;
(World Bank data). Sugar weighs so heavily on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:54.880
the economy that the crown not only staked land to&nbsp;
bolster the sector, but also chose to make itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.520
fiscally attractive to large multinational&nbsp;
investors, mainly The Coca-Cola Company. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:06.080
U.S. company's 2013 financial statements in the&nbsp;
section on taxes show that Brazil, Costa Rica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:11.680
Singapore and Swaziland are four countries&nbsp;
that would have reserved tax incentives for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:16.560
Certainly, however, the fortunes of Coca-Cola's&nbsp;
income statement have not been matched by those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:22.160
who inhabit the country and don't own shares in&nbsp;
the Tibiyo fund, which still proves to be an all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:27.120
too sectoral development tool from which very&nbsp;
few can benefit. Coca-Cola is also active in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:33.280
country today through its subsidiary Conco, which&nbsp;
buys sugar from local producers and processes it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:38.560
directly there to produce the drink concentrate.&nbsp;
After that, according to the report "Swaziland:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:42.560
Southern Africa's Forgotten Crisis" (by Chatman&nbsp;
House), much of the concentrate is then shipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:48.480
to Coca-Cola's bottling plant in Nelspruit, South&nbsp;
Africa. The same report also states that in 2013&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:54.560
Coca-Cola's contribution to Swaziland's economy&nbsp;
would have been between 20 and 40 percent of GDP.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:59.040
As early as 2012, the British newspaper&nbsp;
"The Guardian" raised the issue with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:04.640
titled article "Coca-Cola accused of propping up&nbsp;
notorious Swaziland dictator," and many claimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:10.080
that the U.S. giant's profits weren t helping&nbsp;
the people of eSwatini as the king grew richer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:15.280
and richer. The accusation of helping to secure&nbsp;
profits for the crown had been answered by Sherree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:21.040
Shereni, Coca-Cola's Central Africa spokeswoman,&nbsp;
by stating that "King Mswati III wasn t receiving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:26.800
any profits or dividends from Conco Swaziland."&nbsp;
From Conco maybe not, but Conco is only one part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:31.440
of the multinational's branched presence in the&nbsp;
country. On the other hand, as far as foreign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:36.960
trade is concerned, the largest relations are with&nbsp;
the powerful neighbor South Africa but 20 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:42.400
of exports are to the old continent, so much so&nbsp;
that the European Union has ratified Economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:48.000
Partnership Agreements with the country, centered&nbsp;
mainly on the tax-free export of several products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:52.720
including guess what? sugar. You are then&nbsp;
familiar with China's strategy of penetration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:57.920
into Africa. You know that the Asian nation is&nbsp;
a major trading partner for many African states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:03.520
but still eSwatini remains the white sheep of the&nbsp;
flock, recognizing Taiwan's sovereignty. So as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:08.800
result of the "one China policy," it renounces&nbsp;
diplomatic and trade relations with Beijing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:13.680
I remind you of my old video on tense relations&nbsp;
between Taiwan and China, catch it up if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:19.120
missed it. Things may be changing, however. In&nbsp;
fact, it seems that the Swazi crown will sooner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:24.480
or later want to follow in Nicaragua's footsteps&nbsp;
and remove itself from the list of states that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:29.840
recognize Taiwan's sovereignty, as it moves closer&nbsp;
to the China s policies on the 5G deal. What will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:34.960
become of eSwatini in the near future? Widespread&nbsp;
discontent with the crown shows that something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:40.000
is moving, but how many inhabitants are really&nbsp;
willing to accept change? Could it be the close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:45.120
ties to tradition that prevent the people from&nbsp;
opening their eyes to the bitter reality? Well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:50.560
we are done for today as well, thank you all for&nbsp;
your attention. See you in the next video. Ciao!

